# Cats developer workspace

Generated from `cats-one/templates/workspace/AGENTS.md.template`, the workspace
manifest and member skill inventory. Edit those tracked sources to share changes
with other development machines.

Refresh both agents and check the result from this parent directory:

| OS | Command (no parameters needed) |
| --- | --- |
| Windows | `.\cats-one\scripts\windows\Sync-WorkspaceSkills.ps1` |
| macOS | `./cats-one/scripts/macos/sync-workspace-skills.sh` |
| Linux | `./cats-one/scripts/linux/sync-workspace-skills.sh` |

Add `-WhatIf` on Windows or `--dry-run` on Bash to preview. The explicit
`cats-one/scripts/workspace.mjs` interface supports individual agent targets;
see `cats-one/docs/setup-guide.md`.

## Choose the owning repository

{{MEMBERS}}

Before changing a member, read its `AGENTS.md`, your own agent-specific file when
present (for example, `CODEX.md` for Codex), and relevant member guides. Read only
your own agent-specific file. Apply each member's rules within its scope; a task
spanning repositories can require reading several members' instructions.

Run repository-relative commands with the owning checkout as the working
directory. Each checkout has independent Git state; this parent need not be a
repository. Runtime owns generic product workspace capabilities; platform owns
Desktop/App selection and installation; apps owns utility artifact production.

## Maintenance skills

{{SKILLS}}

Interpret a skill's repository-relative paths and commands in its owning member.
Skill-local resource links remain relative to the skill directory. Edit canonical
sources listed above, then rerun workspace sync after pulling changes.
Runtime's separate product `runtime-skills/` library is outside this developer inventory.

Repository-local sessions retain their own setup. Changing a shell command's
working directory does not reload the active conversation's skills. Reopen a
parent-root session when the agent host requires it to discover changed skills.
